---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import TableOfContents from '../../components/sections/TableOfContents.astro';

const faq = [
	{
		question: 'What is the main difference between a deluge valve and a pre-action valve?',
		answer: 'A deluge system has open sprinklers or nozzles, so opening the deluge valve sends water to every discharge point at once. A pre-action system normally has closed sprinklers and uses detection to open a control valve before a sprinkler releases water. Deluge is selected for rapid, simultaneous coverage; pre-action is selected where accidental water discharge must be limited. Confirm the required interlock and code sequence against the current edition.'
	},
	{
		question: 'Should the deluge valve use electric or hydraulic release?',
		answer: 'Choose the release method from the detection design, hazardous-area requirements, available power and required fail-safe sequence. Electric release is practical when the fire alarm panel and supervised solenoid circuit are already specified. Hydraulic or pneumatic release can suit sites where a dedicated pressure line or pneumatic detector is preferred. Wanlian supplies the valve group and compatible accessories; the detection control panel sequence must be confirmed by the system designer.'
	},
	{
		question: 'How should a deluge system be protected in a cold climate?',
		answer: 'Keep the deluge valve assembly and trim in a heated, accessible space whenever possible, and avoid leaving water-filled exposed pipe where freezing is possible. Provide condensate collection and drainage for the detection trim, insulate vulnerable sections, and use an approved dry or pneumatic arrangement where the design requires it. Do not substitute antifreeze or alter trim without the authority having jurisdiction. Confirm the freeze-protection method against the current edition.'
	},
	{
		question: 'How can the detection line be protected against accidental tripping?',
		answer: 'Separate the detection line from ordinary service controls, supervise its pressure or circuit status, protect exposed devices from impact, and use the releasing panel sequence required for the hazard. A manual release should be guarded but accessible, while test connections should be clearly identified and restored after testing. Hydraulic, pneumatic and electric detection each need their own alarm and fault supervision. The final cause-and-effect matrix should be approved before commissioning.'
	},
	{
		question: 'What information should be included in a deluge valve RFQ?',
		answer: 'Send the hazard description, protected area, design flow and pressure, valve nominal size and end connection, open-head or monitor layout, detection method, required actuation sequence, ambient temperature, drainage arrangement and applicable code edition. Also request trim drawings, material data, pressure test records, spare-part information and operating instructions. If a listing or approval is required, state it explicitly and ask the supplier to confirm the current documentation.'
	},
	{
		question: 'Does Wanlian supply the deluge detection control panel?',
		answer: 'Wanlian supplies deluge valve groups and associated valves and monitoring accessories, including the ZSFM assembly and suitable isolation or control components. We do not present a detection control panel as part of the standard valve package when it is not supplied. The releasing panel, detectors and cause-and-effect programming should be specified with the fire alarm or specialist detection contractor, then checked against the valve trim and site sequence.'
	}
];

const toc = [
	{ id: 'when-deluge', label: '1. When a System Needs Deluge Protection' },
	{ id: 'how-it-works', label: '2. How the ZSFM Deluge Valve Group Works' },
	{ id: 'actuation', label: '3. Choosing the Actuation Method' },
	{ id: 'sizing', label: '4. Sizing the Valve for Flow and Trip Time' },
	{ id: 'discharge', label: '5. Open Sprinklers, Monitors and Discharge Layout' },
	{ id: 'testing', label: '6. Commissioning and Periodic Testing' },
	{ id: 'rfq-checklist', label: '7. Deluge Valve RFQ Checklist' },
	{ id: 'faq', label: 'Frequently Asked Questions' }
];

const products = [
	{ slug: 'deluge-alarm-valve-zsfm', name: 'Deluge Alarm Valve Group (ZSFM Series)', img: '/assets/products/deluge-alarm-valve-zsfm.webp', desc: 'Wet-bottom deluge valve group for open-head and monitor systems' },
	{ slug: 'pre-action-alarm-system-zsfy', name: 'Pre-action Deluge Alarm System (ZSFY Series)', img: '/assets/products/pre-action-alarm-system-zsfy.jpg', desc: 'Single and double interlock pre-action assemblies' },
	{ slug: 'manual-fire-water-cannon-ps', name: 'Manual Fire Water Monitor (PS Series)', img: '/assets/products/manual-fire-water-cannon-ps.webp', desc: 'Fixed monitor for deluge and monitor discharge duty' },
	{ slug: 'ul-fm-resilient-seat-osy-gate-valve', name: 'UL/FM Resilient Seated OS&amp;Y Gate Valve', img: '/assets/products/ul-fm-resilient-seat-osy-gate-valve.webp', desc: '2"-10", grooved ends, rising stem, UL/FM listed' }
];
---

<Layout
	title="Deluge Valve Sizing and Selection Guide (2026)"
	description="How a ZSFM deluge valve works, how to size it for flow and trip time, actuation options, open-head and monitor layouts, testing and RFQ data."
	type="article"
	faq={faq}
	breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Guides', href: '/blog/' }, { name: 'Deluge Valve Selection Guide' }]}
>
	<main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-14 mb-16">
		<nav class="text-xs text-gray-400 font-bold uppercase tracking-widest mb-6 flex gap-2">
			<a href="/" class="hover:text-blue-600">Home</a>
			<span>/</span>
			<a href="/blog/" class="hover:text-blue-600">Guides</a>
			<span>/</span>
			<span class="text-gray-600">Deluge Valve Selection Guide</span>
		</nav>

		<article class="bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-sm border border-gray-100 p-6 sm:p-10">
			<p class="text-brand-red font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-sm mb-3">Deluge Valve Guide</p>
			<h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-black text-gray-900 leading-tight tracking-tight">Deluge Valve Sizing and Selection: Open Sprinkler and Monitor Systems</h1>
			<p class="mt-5 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed text-lg">In short: a deluge valve is the controlled gate between a pressurised water supply and a network of open sprinklers, nozzles or monitors. The detection line operates first; the valve then admits water to all open discharge points simultaneously. Good selection starts with the hazard and design flow, then checks Kv or Cv capacity, pressure loss, trip time, actuation method, drainage and test access. This guide explains the ZSFM group, the comparison with wet, dry and pre-action systems, and the information a buyer should put in an RFQ.</p>

			<TableOfContents headings={toc} />

			<div class="grid grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-4 mb-4">
				{products.map(p => (
					<a href={`/products/${p.slug}/`} class="group bg-gray-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 overflow-hidden hover:border-brand-red hover:shadow-md transition">
						<img src={p.img} alt={p.name} loading="lazy" class="w-full h-28 object-contain bg-white p-2" />
						<div class="p-3">
							<p class="text-xs font-bold text-gray-800 group-hover:text-brand-red leading-snug transition-colors">{p.name}</p>
							<p class="text-[10px] text-gray-400 mt-1 line-clamp-2">{p.desc}</p>
						</div>
					</a>
				))}
			</div>

			<h2 id="when-deluge" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">1. When a System Needs Deluge Protection</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Deluge protection is appropriate when a fire can grow quickly, when the whole hazard must be wetted together, or when a directional monitor must receive water immediately after detection. Typical examples include stage scenery and theatre fly spaces, aircraft hangars, transformers, conveyor belts, vehicle areas and foam-water pre-action applications. The defining feature is that the sprinklers or nozzles are open. Water does not wait for an individual sprinkler bulb to operate; one release sequence sends water across the complete open network.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">The trade-off is deliberate water demand. A deluge designer must consider every open discharge point that can operate at once, the available supply at the valve inlet, pipe friction and the pressure needed at the hydraulically most remote outlet. EN 12845 and NFPA 13 require the deluge valve to supply all open outlets quickly after detection, commonly expressed as about 60 seconds, but the governing edition and project hazard determine the exact design basis. Always confirm the requirement against the current edition.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The decision is not simply “largest valve available.” A wet alarm check valve such as the <a href="/products/wet-alarm-check-valve-zsfz-150/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">ZSFZ wet alarm check valve</a> belongs to a different, normally closed sprinkler arrangement. For an open-head system, the <a href="/products/deluge-alarm-valve-zsfm/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">ZSFM deluge alarm valve group</a> is the relevant starting point, subject to the hydraulic calculation and approval requirements.</p>

			<h2 id="how-it-works" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">2. How the ZSFM Deluge Valve Group Works</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">A deluge valve is held closed by a priming arrangement while the supply side remains pressurised. An independent detection circuit senses the hazard and releases the trim through a hydraulic, pneumatic or electric sequence. Once the holding pressure is removed or the releasing device operates, the main diaphragm or clapper moves and supply water enters the deluge piping. Water then reaches all open sprinklers, nozzles or monitors rather than waiting for thermal operation at each outlet.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">The ZSFM group should be reviewed as an assembly, not only as a body. The RFQ should identify the valve size, inlet and outlet connection, priming trim, drain and test arrangement, alarm outlet, pressure switches and manual emergency release. The alarm path may operate a water motor alarm or transmit pressure to a monitored panel, depending on the specified trim. A supervised isolation valve upstream and a controlled downstream test arrangement make the group maintainable without hiding a closed valve.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Wanlian supplies deluge valve groups with associated valves and monitoring accessories. The detection panel, detectors and programmed cause-and-effect logic are not silently included when they are supplied by another specialist. That boundary should appear on the equipment schedule so the installer knows who is responsible for the releasing circuit, alarm notification and reset sequence.</p>

			<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-gray-200 my-5">
				<table class="min-w-full divide-y divide-gray-200 text-sm">
					<thead class="bg-gray-50">
						<tr>
							<th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wider">System type</th>
							<th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wider">Discharge condition</th>
							<th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wider">Typical selection logic</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody class="bg-white divide-y divide-gray-200">
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Deluge</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">All open outlets flow after the valve trips</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Rapid, simultaneous wetting of a high-hazard area</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Wet pipe</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Only heat-operated sprinklers discharge</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Heated spaces with closed sprinklers</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Dry pipe</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Air releases before water enters the dry network</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Areas where freezing prevents a wet system</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Pre-action</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Detection opens the valve; closed sprinklers still control discharge</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Water-sensitive spaces needing an added release condition</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>

			<h2 id="actuation" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">3. Choosing the Actuation Method</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">The detection line is separate from the water distribution piping and acts before the deluge valve. A liquid-filled hydraulic detection line can release through a pilot device; a pneumatic line can use air pressure and a dry pilot arrangement; an electric release uses a supervised solenoid and releasing panel. The choice depends on the site environment, available utilities, cable routing, supervision philosophy and the required sequence for manual release, alarm and reset.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Electric release is often convenient where detectors, a fire alarm panel and remote status points already exist. Hydraulic or pneumatic release can be preferred where the control philosophy is local or where the detection line must remain independent of a long electrical circuit. The <a href="/products/groove-solenoid-control-gate-valve-gkzf1/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">GKZF1 grooved solenoid control valve</a> may suit an electrically controlled valve arrangement, but the releasing panel output, voltage, supervision and reset method still need to be confirmed by the system designer.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">For a pre-action alternative, the <a href="/products/pre-action-alarm-system-zsfy/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">ZSFY pre-action alarm system</a> provides a useful comparison because single-interlock and double-interlock sequences impose different release conditions. Do not choose by the word “electric” alone: document what happens on loss of power, loss of pilot pressure, detector alarm, manual release, waterflow alarm and restoration. The final matrix should be tested as an operating sequence, not just checked as a wiring diagram.</p>

			<h2 id="sizing" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">4. Sizing the Valve for Flow and Trip Time</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Size the valve from the calculated demand, not only from the branch-pipe diameter. Establish the design flow from all open heads, monitors or nozzles that the hazard requires to operate, then check the available inlet pressure and the remote outlet pressure. The selected valve must pass the required flow with acceptable loss while the trim and alarm path remain within their operating range. Kv or Cv is the useful comparison value, but it must be read at the relevant pressure drop and not copied from an unrelated test condition.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Trip time is a system result. It includes detection response, pilot-line pressure decay or electrical release, valve opening, trim response, pipe filling and water arrival at the remote outlets. EN 12845 and NFPA 13 use rapid-delivery expectations for deluge service, often around 60 seconds to supply the open outlets after detection. Confirm the exact project requirement against the current edition, then validate it with the longest and most hydraulically adverse branch rather than the shortest test connection.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The detection line needs its own calculation. Its longest or most remote pilot path may govern pressure decay, air volume, water displacement or electrical supervision. Include elevation, fittings, condensate points and the actual release device. If cold or humid conditions can create condensation, provide a condensate collector and drain at a suitable low point. These details are easy to omit from a valve-only quotation and are often the reason a site trip test does not match the drawing.</p>

			<h2 id="discharge" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">5. Open Sprinklers, Monitors and Discharge Layout</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Open sprinklers and nozzles are laid out to cover the hazard as a group. Their orifice, spray angle, spacing and elevation affect the hydraulic demand, while obstructions and wind can affect actual coverage. A monitor arrangement adds throw, aiming and reaction considerations; a fixed <a href="/products/manual-fire-water-cannon-ps/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">PS manual fire water monitor</a> can be part of a deluge or monitor discharge duty, but the water supply must be checked for the monitor flow together with any simultaneous open heads.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Provide a downstream isolation arrangement only where the approved design permits it, and supervise any valve that could disable part of the hazard. The deluge drain and test connection should discharge safely without flooding the valve room or sending contaminated water into an unsuitable drain. Keep the alarm outlet visible and accessible, and mark the manual release so an operator can identify it during an emergency without confusing it with the test or drain valve.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">For system context, compare the hydraulic assumptions with the <a href="/guides/esfr-sprinkler-system-guide/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">ESFR sprinkler system guide</a> and the <a href="/guides/fire-sprinkler-system-wet-dry-preaction/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">wet, dry and pre-action system guide</a>. Where foam is added, review the <a href="/guides/foam-proportioning-system-guide/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">foam proportioning system guide</a> so the deluge valve, concentrate equipment and discharge devices are treated as one hydraulic and control sequence.</p>

			<h2 id="testing" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">6. Commissioning and Periodic Testing</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Commissioning should prove both the water path and the release path. Confirm that the upstream supply valve and trim isolation valves are in their intended positions, the priming pressure is stable, the drain is clear and the alarm outlet is connected. Operate the manual release and the normal detection sequence separately where the approved procedure permits, then record the time from release to valve operation, alarm signal and water arrival at the representative remote outlet.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Test the bypass and drain without leaving a hidden route open. Verify the water motor alarm or pressure switch, alarm transmission, supervisory contacts and reset. A flow switch or indicator downstream can support status monitoring; the <a href="/guides/water-flow-indicator-guide/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">water flow indicator guide</a> gives broader context for choosing and supervising that signal. Before the rainy season or other high-risk operating period, perform the planned trip test with the owner, alarm contractor and authority requirements aligned.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Periodic maintenance should follow NFPA 25 or the applicable local maintenance code and the valve manufacturer's instructions. Inspect trim for corrosion, confirm drains and condensate collectors are not blocked, and record pressure readings rather than relying on memory. The test must be controlled so the discharge area is safe and the system is restored promptly. If the current edition changes an interval, acceptance criterion or documentation requirement, confirm against the current edition instead of reusing an old checklist.</p>

			<h2 id="rfq-checklist" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">7. Deluge Valve RFQ Checklist</h2>
			<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
				<li><strong>Describe the hazard.</strong> State the room or process, combustible load, protected footprint and whether the outlets are open sprinklers, nozzles, monitors or foam-water devices.</li>
				<li><strong>Give hydraulic data.</strong> Provide design flow, required remote pressure, available supply pressure, pipe size, elevation and the governing hydraulic calculation assumptions.</li>
				<li><strong>Define the valve group.</strong> State nominal size, end connection, body and trim materials, drain size, alarm outlet, test arrangement and any supervised isolation valve.</li>
				<li><strong>Specify the release.</strong> Identify hydraulic, pneumatic or electric detection, pilot-line length, manual release, solenoid details, panel interface and the required trip sequence.</li>
				<li><strong>Address the environment.</strong> State minimum and maximum ambient temperature, indoor or outdoor location, condensation risk, drainage route and corrosion exposure.</li>
				<li><strong>Request the documents.</strong> Ask for an assembly drawing, trim schematic, installation and maintenance instructions, pressure test record, material information and current approval or listing documents when the project requires them.</li>
				<li><strong>Set the acceptance test.</strong> Define the target trip time, alarm signals, remote outlet observation, restoration procedure and the records required after the trip test.</li>
			</ul>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">For a wider procurement framework, use the <a href="/fire-sprinkler-systems/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">fire sprinkler systems overview</a> alongside the related guides above. A complete RFQ lets Wanlian confirm the valve group without pretending to design the detector layout or the entire fire alarm panel. The result is a clearer division of supply, a more reliable hydraulic check and fewer changes during commissioning.</p>

			<h2 id="faq" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-6 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
			<div class="space-y-4">
